note chain.om: the INTERPLANE ledger, a hash chain anyone can check. Composed after om/tls/sha2.om.
note   chain add LEDGER KIND ACTOR SUBJECT < payload    append one record; prints "OK seq head"
note   chain verify LEDGER                              prints "OK count head" or "BAD line N code"
note   chain check LEDGER SEQ < payload                 the payload is the one record SEQ committed to
note   chain feed LEDGER FILE KIND ACTOR [PAGE]          one record per new complete line of FILE; then
note                                                    the page, when PAGE is given
note   chain page LEDGER OUT                             write OUT: the head as a page with the check by hand
note feed records line N of FILE with the subject "<file name> line N" and the SHA-256 of the line's
note bytes (without its line feed) as payload. It resumes after the highest N already recorded for
note that KIND and file name, so running it again adds only new lines. A last line without a line
note feed is still being written and is left for the next run. Only the digest enters the ledger.
note FILE may not be the ledger itself, by any name (exit 1, nothing written).
note A record is one line of seven tab-separated fields, ended by a line feed:
note   seq  utc  kind  actor  subject  payload  prev
note seq counts from 1. utc is YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ and never goes backwards. payload is the SHA-256
note of the payload bytes, 64 lowercase hex digits. prev is the SHA-256 of the previous record's line
note (its bytes without the line feed), or the word GENESIS in record 1. The head of a ledger is the
note SHA-256 of its last line. Changing, removing, inserting or reordering any record changes the head
note unless a SHA-256 collision is found (Ledger.lean, theorem tamper_evident).
note Without this program: printf %s "LINE" | sha256sum gives the next record's prev.
note add locks the ledger, verifies all of it, and refuses to append to a ledger that does not verify.
note Exit 0 done; 1 usage or a bad argument; 2 the ledger does not verify; 3 read or write failed;
note 4 check: no such record, or the payload is not the one committed; 5 feed: FILE has fewer
note complete lines than were already recorded from it (nothing is written).
note BAD codes: 1 not seven fields, or a byte that is not a tab or printable ASCII; 2 seq (no leading
note zero); 3 utc (a real date and time, never earlier than the record before); 4 payload digest;
note 5 prev; 6 line longer than 1,023 bytes; 7 last line has no line feed; 8 kind, actor or subject
note breaks its rule (kind 1..24 bytes of A-Z 0-9 _, actor 1..64 bytes, subject 1..128 bytes).
note page verifies the ledger first and writes nothing when it does not verify (exit 2). OUT is
note written as OUT.tmp (created exclusively) and renamed, so a reader never sees half a page. A
note page name, or its .tmp, that is the ledger or the fed file is refused (exit 1).
note Commands are matched as whole words: add, verify, check, feed, page.
global gl uchar 1024
global gll ulong 1
global gh uchar 64
global ghl ulong 1
global gph uchar 64
global gq uchar 64
global gu uchar 20
global gs ulong 8
global ga uchar 4096
global gw uchar 2048
global gwl ulong 1
global gb uchar 65536
global gx uchar 512
global gxl ulong 4
text tgen "GENESIS"
text tok "OK\t"
text tbad "BAD\tline\t"
text tadd "add"
text tver "verify"
text tchk "check"
text tfeed "feed"
text tline " line "
text tpage "page"
text ttmp ".tmp"
text tnone "no records yet"
text q01 "<!doctype html><html lang='en' data-theme='dark'><head><meta charset='utf-8'><meta name='viewport' content='width=device-width,initial-scale=1'><title>Check the record yourself - INTERPLANE</title><link rel='stylesheet' href='/assets/tokens.css'><link rel='stylesheet' href='/assets/proof.css'></head><body><main>\n"
text q02 "<p class='eyebrow'>Proof</p><h1>Check the record yourself</h1>\n<p class='sub'>The agents that build INTERPLANE talk on one message board. Each line of that board is recorded here by its SHA-256 digest, and each record names the digest of the record before it. You do not need to trust this page, this site or its author. Download the ledger and check it with tools already on your machine.</p>\n<dl class='stats'><div><dt>Records</dt><dd>"
text q03 "</dd></div><div><dt>Last record (UTC)</dt><dd>"
text q04 "</dd></div></dl>\n<h2>The head</h2>\n<p class='sub'>The head is the SHA-256 of the ledger's last line. Change, remove, add or move any earlier record and the head changes.</p>\n<pre class='cmd'>"
text q05 "</pre>\n<h2>Check it by hand</h2>\n<p class='sub'>Get the ledger and compute its head. It must be the line above. Where there is no sha256sum (macOS), shasum -a 256 does the same.</p>\n<pre class='cmd'>curl -s https://interplane.io/proof/board.tsv -o board.tsv\nprintf %s &quot;$(tail -n 1 board.tsv)&quot; | sha256sum</pre>\n"
text q06 "<p class='sub'>Then check every link. Field 7 of each record must be the SHA-256 of the line above it.</p>\n<pre class='cmd'>awk 'NR&gt;1' board.tsv | cut -f7 &gt; claimed\nsed '$d' board.tsv | while IFS= read -r line; do printf %s &quot;$line&quot; | sha256sum | cut -d' ' -f1; done &gt; computed\ncmp claimed computed &amp;&amp; echo chain intact</pre>\n"
text q07 "<h2>Prove one message</h2>\n<p class='sub'>Only digests are public; the board itself is not. Whoever holds line N of the board, as it was when the line was recorded, can prove it is the line that was recorded: its SHA-256 is field 6 of the record whose subject is BOARD.md line N. Write the line number in place of N; the command reads the line from the file, so nothing in it needs quoting.</p>\n<pre class='cmd'>printf %s &quot;$(sed -n 'Np' BOARD.md)&quot; | sha256sum</pre>\n"
text q08 "<h2>What this proves, and what it does not</h2>\n<p class='sub'>Proved in Lean 4 with no axioms: two valid ledgers with the same head are the same ledger, unless SHA-256 gives one digest for two different lines. Nobody is known to be able to do that; it is assumed, not proved.</p>\n<p class='sub'>Not proved by the chain alone: when a record was written, and who or what wrote a message. A ledger kept on one machine can be rebuilt by whoever holds it. Time comes from outside, from publishing each head where it cannot be taken back.</p>\n</main></body></html>\n"
global gp uchar 16384
global gpl ulong 1
global go uchar 2100
note gs: 0 records accepted, 1 first BAD code, 2 its line number, 3 wanted seq, 4 wanted seq found,
note 5 feed mode, 6 highest source line already recorded for the fed kind and file name
note gx: the fed kind at 0 (length gxl 0), actor at 64 (gxl 1), file name at 192 (gxl 2)
fn hexc v ulong to uchar
if lt v 10
ret uchar add 48 v
end
ret uchar add 87 v
end
note hline: gh = hex SHA-256 of the current line gl
fn hline to void
t = new uchar 1024 0
d = new uchar 32 0
n = get gll 0
i = ulong 0
while lt i n bound 1024
put t i get gl i
i = add i 1
end
k = sha2_digest 256 part t 0 n d
j = ulong 0
while lt j 32 bound 32
b = ulong get d j
put gh mul j 2 hexc shr b 4
put gh add mul j 2 1 hexc and b 15
j = add j 1
end
put ghl 0 64
end
note phash: gph = hex SHA-256 of everything on stdin; false when a read fails
fn phash to bool
c = new ulong 40 0
b = new uchar 65536 0
d = new uchar 32 0
sha256_init c
k = long 1
while gt k 0 bound 9223372036854775807
k = read 0 b 0 65536
if gt k 0
sha256_update c part b 0 ulong k
end
end
sha256_final c d
j = ulong 0
while lt j 32 bound 32
x = ulong get d j
put gph mul j 2 hexc shr x 4
put gph add mul j 2 1 hexc and x 15
j = add j 1
end
ret eq k 0
end
note ---- the current line gl: field k starts at lfs k and ends at lfe of that start
fn lfe a ulong to ulong
n = get gll 0
i = a
while lt i n bound 1024
if eq get gl i 9
ret i
end
i = add i 1
end
ret n
end
fn lfs k ulong to ulong
p = ulong 0
j = ulong 0
while lt j k bound 8
p = add lfe p 1
j = add j 1
end
ret p
end
note lshape: exactly six tabs and every other byte printable ASCII (32..126)
fn lshape to bool
n = get gll 0
t = ulong 0
i = ulong 0
while lt i n bound 1024
c = get gl i
if eq c 9
t = add t 1
else
if or lt c 32 gt c 126
ret false
end
end
i = add i 1
end
ret eq t 6
end
note lnum: the decimal number in gl a..b; 0 when it is not 1 to 18 digits or has a leading zero
fn lnum a ulong b ulong to ulong
if ge a b
ret 0
end
if gt sub b a 18
ret 0
end
if eq get gl a 48
ret 0
end
v = ulong 0
i = a
while lt i b bound 18
c = get gl i
if or lt c 48 gt c 57
ret 0
end
v = add mul v 10 ulong sub c 48
i = add i 1
end
ret v
end
fn ldig i ulong to bool
c = get gl i
ret and ge c 48 le c 57
end
note l2: the two decimal digits at gl a as a number (the caller checked they are digits)
fn l2 a ulong to ulong
x = ulong get gl a
y = ulong get gl add a 1
ret add mul sub x 48 10 sub y 48
end
note ldim: days in month m of year y (proleptic Gregorian)
fn ldim y ulong m ulong to ulong
if eq m 2
if and eq rem y 4 0 or ne rem y 100 0 eq rem y 400 0
ret 29
end
ret 28
end
if or or eq m 4 eq m 6 or eq m 9 eq m 11
ret 30
end
ret 31
end
note lreal: the utc at gl a (digits already checked) names a real date and time
fn lreal a ulong to bool
m = l2 add a 5
if or lt m 1 gt m 12
ret false
end
d = l2 add a 8
y = add mul l2 a 100 l2 add a 2
if or lt d 1 gt d ldim y m
ret false
end
if gt l2 add a 11 23
ret false
end
if gt l2 add a 14 59
ret false
end
if gt l2 add a 17 59
ret false
end
ret true
end
note lword: field a..b is 1..cap bytes; with up set, each byte is A-Z, 0-9 or _
fn lword a ulong b ulong cap ulong up bool to bool
if or eq a b gt sub b a cap
ret false
end
if not up
ret true
end
i = a
while lt i b bound 1024
c = get gl i
ok = false
if and ge c 65 le c 90
ok = true
end
if and ge c 48 le c 57
ok = true
end
if eq c 95
ok = true
end
if not ok
ret false
end
i = add i 1
end
ret true
end
note lutc: field a..b is YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ and not before the last accepted record's
fn lutc a ulong b ulong to bool
if ne sub b a 20
ret false
end
i = ulong 0
while lt i 20 bound 20
c = get gl add a i
w = uchar 0
if eq i 4
w = uchar 45
end
if eq i 7
w = uchar 45
end
if eq i 10
w = uchar 84
end
if eq i 13
w = uchar 58
end
if eq i 16
w = uchar 58
end
if eq i 19
w = uchar 90
end
if eq w 0
if not ldig add a i
ret false
end
else
if ne c w
ret false
end
end
i = add i 1
end
if not lreal a
ret false
end
if gt get gs 0 0
j = ulong 0
while lt j 20 bound 20
x = get gl add a j
y = get gu j
if gt x y
ret true
end
if lt x y
ret false
end
j = add j 1
end
end
ret true
end
fn lhex a ulong b ulong to bool
if ne sub b a 64
ret false
end
i = a
while lt i b bound 64
c = get gl i
ok = false
if and ge c 48 le c 57
ok = true
end
if and ge c 97 le c 102
ok = true
end
if not ok
ret false
end
i = add i 1
end
ret true
end
note lprev: field a..b equals the expected prev (gh, ghl bytes)
fn lprev a ulong b ulong to bool
n = get ghl 0
if ne sub b a n
ret false
end
i = ulong 0
while lt i n bound 64
if ne get gl add a i get gh i
ret false
end
i = add i 1
end
ret true
end
note lcheck: 0 when the current line is the next valid record, else its BAD code
fn lcheck to ulong
if not lshape
ret 1
end
if ne lnum 0 lfe 0 add get gs 0 1
ret 2
end
u = lfs 1
if not lutc u lfe u
ret 3
end
k = lfs 2
if not lword k lfe k 24 true
ret 8
end
k = lfs 3
if not lword k lfe k 64 false
ret 8
end
k = lfs 4
if not lword k lfe k 128 false
ret 8
end
p = lfs 5
if not lhex p lfe p
ret 4
end
q = lfs 6
if not lprev q lfe q
ret 5
end
ret 0
end
note laccept: the current line passed lcheck; remember its time, its digest, and the wanted record
fn laccept to void
u = lfs 1
i = ulong 0
while lt i 20 bound 20
put gu i get gl add u i
i = add i 1
end
n = add get gs 0 1
if eq n get gs 3
p = lfs 5
j = ulong 0
while lt j 64 bound 64
put gq j get gl add p j
j = add j 1
end
put gs 4 1
end
ltrack
hline
put gs 0 n
end
fn lbad code ulong to void
if eq get gs 1 0
put gs 1 code
put gs 2 add get gs 0 1
end
end
fn lend to void
if eq get gs 1 0
c = lcheck
if eq c 0
laccept
else
lbad c
end
end
put gll 0 0
end
note vfy: read the whole ledger from fd. false when a read fails; gs 1 holds the first BAD code.
fn vfy fd int to bool
put gs 0 0
put gs 1 0
put gs 4 0
put gll 0 0
put ghl 0 7
for g ulong 7
put gh g get tgen g
end
k = long 1
while gt k 0 bound 9223372036854775807
k = read fd gb 0 65536
i = ulong 0
while lt long i k bound 65536
c = get gb i
if eq c 10
lend
else
m = get gll 0
if lt m 1023
put gl m c
put gll 0 add m 1
else
lbad 6
end
end
i = add i 1
end
end
if gt get gll 0 0
lbad 7
end
ret eq k 0
end
note ---- output line gw
fn wab c uchar to void
o = get gwl 0
if lt o 2047
put gw o c
put gwl 0 add o 1
end
end
fn wa t view uchar * to void
for i ulong len t bound 64
wab get t i
end
end
fn wan v ulong to void
d = new uchar 20 0
n = ulong 0
x = v
if eq x 0
wab 48
end
while gt x 0 bound 20
put d n uchar add 48 rem x 10
x = div x 10
n = add n 1
end
while gt n 0 bound 20
n = sub n 1
wab get d n
end
end
fn wa2 v ulong to void
wab uchar add 48 rem div v 10 10
wab uchar add 48 rem v 10
end
fn whead to void
n = get ghl 0
i = ulong 0
while lt i n bound 64
wab get gh i
i = add i 1
end
end
note wymd: days since 1970-01-01 as YYYY-MM-DD (proleptic Gregorian; the gate checks it against date -u)
fn wymd days ulong to void
z = add days 719468
era = div z 146097
doe = sub z mul era 146097
t = sub doe div doe 1460
t = add t div doe 36524
t = sub t div doe 146096
yoe = div t 365
y = add yoe mul era 400
doy = sub doe sub add mul 365 yoe div yoe 4 div yoe 100
mp = div add mul 5 doy 2 153
d = add sub doy div add mul 153 mp 2 5 1
m = add mp 3
if ge mp 10
m = sub mp 9
end
if le m 2
y = add y 1
end
wan y
wab 45
wa2 m
wab 45
wa2 d
end
fn wutc s ulong to void
r = rem s 86400
wymd div s 86400
wab 84
wa2 div r 3600
wab 58
wa2 div rem r 3600 60
wab 58
wa2 rem r 60
wab 90
end
note warg: append argument idx; false unless it is 1..cap bytes, each printable ASCII. With up
note set, only A-Z, 0-9 and _ pass (the kind field).
fn warg idx ulong cap ulong up bool to bool
n = argread idx ga 0 4096
if lt n 1
ret false
end
if gt ulong n cap
ret false
end
i = ulong 0
while lt i ulong n bound 4096
c = get ga i
if or lt c 32 gt c 126
ret false
end
if up
ok = false
if and ge c 65 le c 90
ok = true
end
if and ge c 48 le c 57
ok = true
end
if eq c 95
ok = true
end
if not ok
ret false
end
end
wab c
i = add i 1
end
ret true
end
fn wout fd int to bool
n = get gwl 0
o = ulong 0
while lt o n bound 2048
k = write fd gw o sub n o
if le k 0
ret false
end
o = add o ulong k
end
ret true
end
note ropen: open the ledger named by argument 2 with flags; the descriptor or a negative errno
fn ropen flags int to int
n = argread 2 ga 0 4095
if lt n 1
ret -1
end
ret int fopen ga 0 ulong n flags 420
end
note argis: argument 1 is exactly the word t
fn argis t view uchar * to bool
n = argread 1 ga 0 16
if ne n long len t
ret false
end
for i ulong len t bound 16
if ne get ga i get t i
ret false
end
end
ret true
end
note say: print OK count head, or BAD line N code; the exit status that goes with it
fn say to int
put gwl 0 0
if ne get gs 1 0
wa tbad
wan get gs 2
wab 9
wan get gs 1
wab 10
r = wout 1
ret 2
end
wa tok
wan get gs 0
wab 9
whead
wab 10
if wout 1
ret 0
end
ret 3
end
fn cverify to int
fd = ropen 0
if lt fd 0
ret 3
end
ok = vfy fd
r = fclose fd
if not ok
ret 3
end
ret say
end
fn ccheck to int
n = argread 3 ga 0 32
if lt n 1
ret 1
end
i = ulong 0
while lt i ulong n bound 32
put gl i get ga i
i = add i 1
end
put gll 0 ulong n
want = lnum 0 ulong n
if eq want 0
ret 1
end
fd = ropen 0
if lt fd 0
ret 3
end
put gs 3 want
ok = vfy fd
r = fclose fd
if not ok
ret 3
end
if ne get gs 1 0
ret say
end
if eq get gs 4 0
ret 4
end
if not phash
ret 3
end
j = ulong 0
while lt j 64 bound 64
if ne get gph j get gq j
ret 4
end
j = add j 1
end
ret say
end
fn cadd to int
fd = ropen 1090
if lt fd 0
ret 3
end
if ne flock fd 2 0
ret 3
end
if not vfy fd
ret 3
end
if ne get gs 1 0
r = fclose fd
ret say
end
if not phash
ret 3
end
put gwl 0 0
wan add get gs 0 1
wab 9
wutc div ulong realns 1000000000
wab 9
if not warg 3 24 true
ret 1
end
wab 9
if not warg 4 64 false
ret 1
end
wab 9
if not warg 5 128 false
ret 1
end
wab 9
for i ulong 64
wab get gph i
end
wab 9
whead
r = wcommit fd
if ne r 0
ret r
end
x = fclose fd
ret say
end
note wcommit: the line built in gw becomes the next record: it must itself be the next valid
note record, then it is accepted and written. add syncs it here; feed (gs 5) syncs once, after its
note last record, in cfeed. 0, or the exit code.
fn wcommit fd int to int
n = get gwl 0
if gt n 1023
ret 1
end
for k ulong n bound 1024
put gl k get gw k
end
put gll 0 n
if ne lcheck 0
ret 1
end
laccept
wab 10
if not wout fd
ret 3
end
if eq get gs 5 0
if ne fsync fd 0
ret 3
end
end
ret 0
end
note ---- feed
fn kch c uchar to bool
if and ge c 65 le c 90
ret true
end
if and ge c 48 le c 57
ret true
end
ret eq c 95
end
note sarg: argument idx, 1..cap bytes of printable ASCII (with up only A-Z 0-9 _), kept at gx off with
note its length in gxl slot. For slot 2 only the part after the last "/" is kept (the file name).
fn sarg idx ulong off ulong slot ulong cap ulong up bool to bool
n = argread idx ga 0 4096
if lt n 1
ret false
end
a = ulong 0
if eq slot 2
i = ulong 0
while lt i ulong n bound 4096
if eq get ga i 47
a = add i 1
end
i = add i 1
end
end
m = sub ulong n a
if or lt m 1 gt m cap
ret false
end
j = ulong 0
while lt j m bound 128
c = get ga add a j
if or lt c 32 gt c 126
ret false
end
if up
if not kch c
ret false
end
end
put gx add off j c
j = add j 1
end
put gxl slot m
ret true
end
note ltrack: in feed mode, when the accepted line has the fed kind and the subject
note "<file name> line N", remember the largest N
fn ltrack to void
if eq get gs 5 0
ret
end
k = lfs 2
if ne sub lfe k k get gxl 0
ret
end
i = ulong 0
while lt i get gxl 0 bound 24
if ne get gl add k i get gx i
ret
end
i = add i 1
end
s = lfs 4
e = lfe s
nl = get gxl 2
if lt sub e s add nl 7
ret
end
j = ulong 0
while lt j nl bound 128
if ne get gl add s j get gx add 192 j
ret
end
j = add j 1
end
p = add s nl
t = ulong 0
while lt t 6 bound 6
if ne get gl add p t get tline t
ret
end
t = add t 1
end
v = lnum add p 6 e
if gt v get gs 6
put gs 6 v
end
end
fn fhex d view uchar 32 to void
j = ulong 0
while lt j 32 bound 32
x = ulong get d j
put gph mul j 2 hexc shr x 4
put gph add mul j 2 1 hexc and x 15
j = add j 1
end
end
note frec: one record for source line n, whose digest is in gph. 0, or the exit code.
fn frec fd int n ulong to int
put gwl 0 0
wan add get gs 0 1
wab 9
wutc div ulong realns 1000000000
wab 9
i = ulong 0
while lt i get gxl 0 bound 24
wab get gx i
i = add i 1
end
wab 9
j = ulong 0
while lt j get gxl 1 bound 64
wab get gx add 64 j
j = add j 1
end
wab 9
k = ulong 0
while lt k get gxl 2 bound 128
wab get gx add 192 k
k = add k 1
end
wa tline
wan n
wab 9
for h ulong 64
wab get gph h
end
wab 9
whead
ret wcommit fd
end
note floop: record every complete line of sf after line gs 6. 0, or the exit code.
fn floop fd int sf int to int
c = new ulong 40 0
b = new uchar 65536 0
d = new uchar 32 0
start = get gs 6
n = ulong 0
sha256_init c
k = long 1
while gt k 0 bound 9223372036854775807
k = read sf b 0 65536
if gt k 0
s = ulong 0
i = ulong 0
while lt i ulong k bound 65536
if eq get b i 10
n = add n 1
if gt n start
if gt i s
sha256_update c part b s sub i s
end
sha256_final c d
fhex d
r = frec fd n
if ne r 0
ret r
end
sha256_init c
end
s = add i 1
end
i = add i 1
end
if ge n start
if lt s ulong k
sha256_update c part b s sub ulong k s
end
end
end
end
if lt k 0
ret 3
end
if lt n start
ret 5
end
ret 0
end
note fsame: 1 when descriptors a and b name one file (equal st_dev and st_ino, bytes 0..15 of the
note 128-byte stat), 0 when they do not, 3 when fstat fails. Feeding the ledger into itself would
note record every record it writes, and the file would never end.
fn fsame a int b int to int
s = new uchar 128 0
t = new uchar 128 0
if lt fstat a s 0 128 0
ret 3
end
if lt fstat b t 0 128 0
ret 3
end
for i ulong 16
if ne get s i get t i
ret 0
end
end
ret 1
end
fn cfeed to int
if not sarg 4 0 0 24 true
ret 1
end
if not sarg 5 64 1 64 false
ret 1
end
if not sarg 3 192 2 100 false
ret 1
end
if eq argcnt 7
pl = argread 6 ga 0 4095
if or lt pl 1 gt pl 1000
ret 1
end
end
m = argread 3 ga 0 4095
sf = int fopen ga 0 ulong m 0 0
if lt sf 0
ret 3
end
fd = ropen 1090
if lt fd 0
ret 3
end
if ne flock fd 2 0
ret 3
end
r = fsame sf fd
if ne r 0
ret r
end
if eq argcnt 7
r = pprep 6
if ne r 0
ret r
end
end
put gs 5 1
put gs 6 0
if not vfy fd
ret 3
end
if ne get gs 1 0
ret say
end
r = floop fd sf
x = fclose sf
if ne fsync fd 0
ret 3
end
if ne r 0
ret r
end
if eq argcnt 7
pbuild
w = pfile
if ne w 0
ret w
end
end
y = fclose fd
ret say
end
note ---- page
note pb: one byte into the page; a page that would pass 16,383 bytes is marked as over (gpl 16384)
note and pput refuses to write it, so it is never cut short in silence
fn pb c uchar to void
o = get gpl 0
if lt o 16383
put gp o c
put gpl 0 add o 1
else
put gpl 0 16384
end
end
note pt: a text of at most 4,096 bytes into the page
fn pt t view uchar * to void
n = len t
i = ulong 0
while lt i n bound 4096
pb get t i
i = add i 1
end
end
note pn: a count with its digits grouped in threes
fn pn v ulong to void
d = new uchar 20 0
n = ulong 0
x = v
if eq x 0
pb 48
end
while gt x 0 bound 20
put d n uchar add 48 rem x 10
x = div x 10
n = add n 1
end
while gt n 0 bound 20
n = sub n 1
pb get d n
if gt n 0
if eq rem n 3 0
pb 44
end
end
end
end
note pbuild: the page for the ledger just verified (gs 0 records, head in gh, last time in gu)
fn pbuild to void
put gpl 0 0
pt q01
pt q02
pn get gs 0
pt q03
if eq get gs 0 0
pt tnone
else
for i ulong 10
pb get gu i
end
pb 32
for j ulong 8
pb get gu add 11 j
end
end
pt q04
if eq get gs 0 0
pt tnone
else
for k ulong 64
pb get gh k
end
end
pt q05
pt q06
pt q07
pt q08
end
note pput: the page into the new file named go[0, n), mode 0644, created exclusively (O_WRONLY,
note O_CREAT, O_EXCL: never through a link, never into a file that is already there); true when all
note of it is written and synced
fn pput n ulong to bool
if gt get gpl 0 16383
ret false
end
fd = int fopen go 0 n 193 420
if lt fd 0
ret false
end
m = get gpl 0
o = ulong 0
good = true
while lt o m bound 16384
k = write fd gp o sub m o
if le k 0
good = false
o = m
else
o = add o ulong k
end
end
if ne fsync fd 0
good = false
end
r = fclose fd
ret good
end
note peq: the first 16 bytes of two stat records (st_dev, st_ino) are equal
fn peq a view uchar 128 b view uchar 128 to bool
for i ulong 16
if ne get a i get b i
ret false
end
end
ret true
end
note pid: s holds the lstat of a name; 1 when it is the entry or the file argument idx names (by
note lstat or by stat), 0 when not, 3 when argument idx cannot be read or does not exist
fn pid s view uchar 128 idx ulong to int
m = argread idx ga 0 4095
if lt m 1
ret 3
end
t = new uchar 128 0
if lt stat ga 0 ulong m t 0 128 0
ret 3
end
if peq s t
ret 1
end
if lt lstat ga 0 ulong m t 0 128 0
ret 3
end
if peq s t
ret 1
end
ret 0
end
note pguard: 0 when the name go[0, n) does not exist or is neither the ledger (argument 2) nor, in
note feed, the fed file (argument 3); otherwise the exit code. Renaming the page over the ledger, or
note removing a stale <name>.tmp that is the ledger, would destroy it.
fn pguard n ulong to int
s = new uchar 128 0
if lt lstat go 0 n s 0 128 0
ret 0
end
r = pid s 2
if ne r 0
ret r
end
if eq argcnt 7
ret pid s 3
end
ret 0
end
note pprep: argument idx (1..1,000 bytes) into go as <name>.tmp, its length in gs 7, and both names
note through pguard. Runs before anything is written. 0, or the exit code.
fn pprep idx ulong to int
n = argread idx ga 0 4095
if or lt n 1 gt n 1000
ret 1
end
l = ulong n
for i ulong l bound 1000
put go i get ga i
end
for j ulong 4
put go add l j get ttmp j
end
put gs 7 l
r = pguard l
if ne r 0
ret r
end
ret pguard add l 4
end
note pfile: the page built by pbuild into the name pprep prepared, written as <name>.tmp and
note renamed. A stale <name>.tmp is removed first (the name only, never what it points to); on any
note failure the .tmp is removed again. w holds the two names as the list renames takes: count 2,
note then each name as a 2-byte length and its bytes. 0, or the exit code.
fn pfile to int
l = get gs 7
t = add l 4
u = unlink go 0 t
if not pput t
u = unlink go 0 t
ret 3
end
w = new uchar 2100 0
put w 0 2
put w 1 0
put w 2 uchar rem t 256
put w 3 uchar div t 256
for a ulong t bound 1004
put w add 4 a get go a
end
b = add 4 t
put w b uchar rem l 256
put w add b 1 uchar div l 256
for c ulong l bound 1000
put w add add b 2 c get go c
end
if ne renames w 0 add add b 2 l 0 0
u = unlink go 0 t
ret 3
end
ret 0
end
fn cpage to int
fd = ropen 0
if lt fd 0
ret 3
end
note the same exclusive lock add and feed take: one page writer per ledger, and never mid-append
if ne flock fd 2 0
ret 3
end
p = pprep 3
if ne p 0
ret p
end
good = vfy fd
r = fclose fd
if not good
ret 3
end
if ne get gs 1 0
ret say
end
pbuild
w = pfile
if ne w 0
ret w
end
ret say
end
fn main to int
if lt argcnt 3
ret 1
end
if argis tadd
if eq argcnt 6
ret cadd
end
ret 1
end
if argis tver
if eq argcnt 3
ret cverify
end
ret 1
end
if argis tchk
if eq argcnt 4
ret ccheck
end
ret 1
end
if argis tfeed
if or eq argcnt 6 eq argcnt 7
ret cfeed
end
ret 1
end
if argis tpage
if eq argcnt 4
ret cpage
end
ret 1
end
ret 1
end
